notes               = "Untitled in source; dated تهران ۱۳۶۸ (Tehran, 1989). هذیان = 'delirium' (fever/shell-shock). Hinges on the symmetry نفرت از جنگ / نفرت از صلح (hatred of war in the eyes, of peace in the teeth). Shares the drained-palette imagery of the page-67 poem and the پناهگاه (shelter) of the page-60 poem. Imported as a draft with machine and lantern layers. Corrected (July 2026): this poem's ===translation=== held an agent rendering, staged there by the pages 61-69 bulk import. Per CLAUDE.md that layer is Ben's alone, so the rendering is now the ===lantern=== and ===translation=== is empty again, superseding the import's earlier and rougher lantern (kept in git history). date_translated is cleared for the same reason — it recorded 6/28/26, the date of the agent pass. **English title 'Shell Shock' is the translator's, at Ben's direction (July 2026) — it is not in the Persian**, which is untitled; هذیان بعد از جنگ is the first line. The cost is worth recording: هذیان recurs four times in the book (the drowsy delirium of the alleys in dark-city, the fevered raving of horse-hooves, talking in one's sleep in letter-alizadeh) and 'shell shock' cannot carry those, so this rendering gains a punch and breaks a thread. Ben's direction (July 2026): the first line is a line and not a title — the book carries hardly any titles, and he titles poems himself while working, so 'Shell Shock' is his English title over an untitled Persian poem and persian_title stays empty. And the English is not to mimic the Persian's punctuation — it should breathe on its own — so the lantern is punctuated for English even though the Persian body carries no commas and one full stop. What the lantern does keep from the Persian: the refrain repeated word for word (the perseveration is the symptom), the bare plurals in 'in eyes / in teeth', and the آه/نگاه rhyme as sigh/eye."
draft               = true

===persian===

هذیان بعد از جنگ...
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
و اشک‌های تو
سبز کم رنگ بودند.
رنگ جنگ در آه می‌ماند در نگاه می‌ماند
من نفرت از جنگ را در چشم‌ها دیده‌ام
و نفرت از صلح را در دندان‌ها
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
می‌دانم چشم‌هایت در مهتاب زیباترند
اما...
باید به پناهگاه برگردیم

تهران ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

Delirium after war...
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
And your tears
Would be pale green.
The color of war remains in a sigh, remains in a gaze
I have seen the hatred of war in eyes
And the hatred of peace in teeth
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight
But...
We must return to the shelter

Tehran 1368

===lantern===
Shell shock, after the war...
If the clouds were not grey,
the rain would be another colour,
and your tears
would be pale green.
The colour of war stays in the sigh, stays in the eye.
I have seen the hatred of war in eyes,
and the hatred of peace in teeth.
If the clouds were not grey,
the rain would be another colour.
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight,
but...
we must go back to the shelter.

Tehran, 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===
هذیان is the raving of fever or shell-shock, and بعد از جنگ dates it: ۱۳۶۸ is 1989, a year after the
August 1988 ceasefire. **So there is no raid.** The poem announces itself as a symptom and then
performs one — the counterfactual comes back word for word, the way a delirious man repeats himself,
and the closing line is a reflex firing after the danger has stopped. What breaks off the tenderness
is not a siren but the war still running inside him. پناهگاه is the same bomb-shelter as the page-60
poem, which was written while the shelter was still needed.

**Why the rain would be a different colour.** The counterfactual is a painter's, and it is doing more
than it appears to. Rain has no colour of its own; it takes the sky's — which is why rain against an
overcast looks grey and rain at sunset looks gold. Tears are rain on a face, so they borrow too. The
chain runs: the clouds are grey, so the rain is grey, so her weeping is grey — and if the sky were
anything else, the same tears would come out سبز کم‌رنگ, a pale green, which in this book is the
colour of longing (page 88, "the green of longing"). He is saying that grief takes its colour from
the weather and the weather is war: under this sky there is not even longing left in her crying. And
he cannot change the sky. He is a painter, so the only way he has of saying *the war has ruined us*
is to say *the grey has ruined the rain* — which the next line then states outright: رنگ جنگ در آه
می‌ماند, the colour of war stays.

The Persian turns to sound at exactly the point where it states that finding. رنگ جنگ (*rang-e jang*,
"the colour of war") rhymes inside itself, and the line then closes on آه … نگاه (*âh … negâh*) —
four rhyming syllables in one line. English can have one of the two: *stays in the sigh, stays in the
eye*, taking نگاه as the eye rather than the gaze, which the next line's چشم‌ها is about to do
regardless.

**Why teeth.** Persian puts almost nothing about speech in the teeth: talk lives in the tongue
(زبان) and the mouth (دهان), and gossip is a thing done behind a back, not between molars. What lives
in the teeth is force. دندان قروچه کردن is to grind them in rage; دندان نشان دادن, to bare them, is to
threaten; دندان تیز کردن, to sharpen them, is to be hungry for a thing one intends to take; and
دندان روی جگر گذاشتن — to set one's tooth on one's liver — is to bite down and endure what cannot be
said aloud. So the line is not about smiling, and not about talk. It is about appetite and clench:
what people are ready to do, and what they are holding in. Eyes are where a thing is received and can
be read off a face; teeth are where it is done. Taken as one face, the eyes have had enough of the war
and the mouth has not — which is colder than either half by itself, and it is why the poem insists he
has *seen* both. (The ceasefire of 1988 was not universally welcome in Iran, which is the ground this
line stands on; the poem names no one.) English gets an accidental gift here: *in the teeth of* already
means *in defiance of*, so "the hatred of peace in teeth" carries a second sense the Persian did not
plant but does not mind.

چشم‌ها and دندان‌ها are bare plurals — "in eyes," "in teeth," with no possessive. Not *their* eyes,
not *our* teeth: an anatomy, and a diagnosis. Eyes are where a thing is received and teeth are where
it is bared, so the body is split, one half sick of the war and the other half unable to stop. That
someone should hate *the peace*, a year after it arrived, is the coldest line in the book.

**And the grey has ash in it.** خاکستری is خاکستر — ash — with an adjective ending: Persian's ordinary
word for the colour is *ash-coloured*, the way English "orange" is a fruit nobody tastes any more.
Two things make that worth saying here. The poem's subject is what war leaves behind, and ash is
precisely residue. And خاکستر is built on خاک, clay — the word this book renders "clay," and the word
standing in its own title, بوی کاهگل, the smell of straw-and-clay. Adobe is what the walls are made
of; ash is what adobe becomes when the house burns. The smell the book is named for and the colour
the war leaves behind are the same material, before and after fire.

*Ash-grey* for خاکستری was weighed and not taken. It is defensible on the ground just given, but the
poem wants to make its own reveal: for five lines this may be weather, and then line six tells you it
is war. Ash in the second line arrives with the answer already inside it. Grey is also flat, and
flatness is how delirium sounds — every other word in this poem is ordinary (clouds, rain, tears,
eyes, teeth, moonlight, shelter), so a compound would be the one literary thing on the page. The
etymology is here instead, for whoever wants it. خاکستری is also the poet's own colour, loved outright
on page 76 ("I love the greys, the greys…") and turned against him here.
